  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I didn't open the dashboard to look at the charts, and I haven't seen the rule run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web access-log data. The fields I used are `timestamp`, `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `extension`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-798b293d Web logs overview\"** has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days:\n- **Total requests:** a single-number metric of the request count.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of request count by `timestamp`.\n- **Responses by status code:** a pie chart on `response.keyword`.\n- **Average bytes by extension:** a bar chart of average `bytes` per `extension.keyword`.\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-798b293d-web-logs-overview-63c0e0cb66be\n\n**Rule \"EFF-798b293d 5xx spike\"** (id `85406fc6-936d-43d2-9bab-182eb71ebeeb`):\n- It is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It matches `response.keyword` against the regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`, and fires when more than 10 matching documents arrive in a 5-minute window.\n- It has no action attached, so when it fires it will only show up as an alert in Kibana. It won't send a notification anywhere.\n- It was created disabled, so I enabled it.",
